<div class="aboutProjectSection1024">
    <h1>AI Development Plan</h1>
    <div class="sectionText">
        <span class="aiIdpSubtitle">How I designed an AI scenario that turns skill data or an employee&rsquo;s own request into an individual development plan based on the 70-20-10 model.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="aiIdpSummary">
        <div class="aiIdpSummaryItem">
            <h5>Problem</h5>
            <span>Employees had to identify their own growth areas, turn them into goals, and split actions by the 70-20-10 model. The process was cognitively heavy and took a lot of time.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="aiIdpSummaryItem">
            <h5>Task</h5>
            <span>We needed to add goal generation to an existing IDP service without changing how development plans were stored or edited.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="aiIdpSummaryItem">
            <h5>My role</h5>
            <span>I shaped the product hypothesis, designed the user flow, neural pipeline, system prompts, and interface, and led the project through launch.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="aiIdpSummaryItem">
            <h5>Result</h5>
            <span>The feature launched as a pilot. More than 1,000 employees opened the AI scenario, and over 200 added generated goals to their development plans.</span>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<section class="aiIdpBackground" aria-labelledby="aiIdpBackgroundTitle">
    <div class="aiIdpBackgroundIntro">
        <h4 id="aiIdpBackgroundTitle">A little about the Development Plan</h4>
        <p>Before adding AI, I worked on redesigning the core individual development plan service. The IDP helps employees set goals, plan their development using the 70-20-10 model, track progress, and connect their tasks to professional skills. My goal was to make the plan clear and easy to use, from creating goals to managing tasks and tracking results.</p>
        <img class="largeImageShadow" src="projects/ai-idp/ai-interface.png" alt="Individual Development Plan service interface">
    </div>
    <div class="aiIdpBackgroundDetails">
        <div class="aiIdpBackgroundCopy">
            <div class="aiIdpBackgroundStory">
                <h4>A problem I knew well</h4>
                <p>As a mentor to designers and a member of the professional community, I regularly heard complaints about the existing IDP. Employees usually put their plans together in one-on-one meetings with their managers, recorded goals and tasks in Confluence or other tools, and then tried to fit them into the bank&rsquo;s recommended 70-20-10 model.</p>
                <p>Goals changed along the way; tasks were moved, added, and marked complete. I understood these workflows from talking directly with users. The need was clear: create one place for flexible plan editing, collaboration with a manager or mentor, and future integration with other HR services.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="aiIdpBackgroundStory">
                <h4>A flexible editor instead of rigid forms</h4>
                <p>Despite limited resources and the project&rsquo;s relatively low priority, we chose a Notion-like editor. It preserved the structure an IDP needs without restricting how people work. Users could freely edit text, drag goals and tasks, delete or duplicate items, mark them complete, and move structured content with copy and paste.</p>
                <p>Thoughtful UI brought these features together in a clear, flexible interface. Users responded positively, and after the redesign both development plan coverage and the annual customer experience index (ICX) increased.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <img src="projects/ai-idp/goal.png" alt="Goal and task editor in the Development Plan">
    </div>
</section>
<section class="aiIdpTransition" aria-labelledby="aiIdpTransitionTitle">
    <h4 id="aiIdpTransitionTitle">But a good interface was not enough</h4>
    <p>Even with a clear interface, employees still had to identify their own growth areas, formulate goals, and come up with concrete actions to achieve them. The redesign made it easier to work with a plan, but it could not do that thinking for them.</p>
    <p>I framed this as a cognitive load problem and connected it to coverage: the more independent thinking a plan requires, the more likely employees are to fill it in mechanically or avoid creating one altogether.</p>
    <p>That led to a new hypothesis: what if we helped employees create the content of their plans, not just manage them? This is how the idea for the AI Development Plan emerged.</p>
</section>
<img src="projects/ai-idp/ai-idb.png" class="projectImage" alt="AI Development Plan cover">
<div class="aboutProjectSection1024">
    <div class="sectionText sectionTextAdditionalYellow aiIdpCallout">
        <span>This is a hybrid case: product work, UX scenario design, and AI solution design.</span>
        <span>AI was used here to lower the threshold for creating an IDP.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="sectionTextAndImageRight">
        <div class="sectionTextAndImageText sectionTextOneThird">
            <h4>First decision: keep the existing IDP intact</h4>
            <span>I decided not to build a new product from scratch. A simpler and more reliable path was to generate a draft for the existing IDP.</span>
            <span>The neural network takes on the most difficult part: it formulates goals, writes descriptions, and suggests tasks. Once the employee saves the result, the draft becomes a regular development plan that can be edited manually.</span>
            <span>This approach reduced development risk and cost: the bank&rsquo;s back end continued to work as before, while AI handled only the first and hardest step.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="sectionTextAndImageImg">
            <img class="aiIdpRoundedImage" src="projects/ai-idp/idb.png" alt="Generated AI Development Plan result">
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="sectionTextAndImageLeft">
        <div class="sectionTextAndImageImg">
            <img class="aiIdpRoundedImage" src="projects/ai-idp/pipeline.png" alt="AI pipeline diagram">
        </div>
        <div class="sectionTextAndImageText sectionTextOneThird">
            <h4>Second decision: split generation into steps</h4>
            <span>At first I wanted to send one request to the model, but that process was hard to control. I split the task between two AI agents. The first acts as a career assistant: it analyzes skills and suggests growth focuses.</span>
            <span>A glue layer then combines these focuses with the user&rsquo;s settings. After that, the second agent assembles the final plan using the 70-20-10 model.</span>
            <span>To make the generated text land in the right fields automatically, I defined a strict response format for the second agent: title, description, group_70, group_20, and group_10.</span>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="sectionTextAndImageRight">
        <div class="sectionTextAndImageText sectionTextOneThird">
            <h4>Settings became a visual prompt</h4>
            <span>I designed two scenarios: generation based on SkillsTrack data, the internal skill assessment system, and a free-form scenario where the employee defines the direction. The plan can then be tuned with preferences, difficulty, learning formats, and a focus on hard or soft skills.</span>
            <span>The same multi-step pipeline works here; only part of the cascade changes.</span>
            <span>I avoided one large text field so the model would not receive unnecessary context. Instead, I used clear controls in the interface. They show users what can be configured and pass precise parameters to the agent.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="sectionTextAndImageImg">
            <img class="aiIdpRoundedImage" src="projects/ai-idp/modal.png" alt="AI Development Plan settings modal">
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="sectionText sectionTextAdditionalYellow aiIdpCallout">
        <span>After launch, the free-form path proved noticeably more popular.</span>
        <span>Approximately 180 plans were added without SkillsTrack, compared with about 20 using SkillsTrack.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="aiIdpQuality">
        <img class="aiIdpQualityImage aiIdpRoundedImage" src="projects/ai-idp/error.png" alt="Error message shown when goal generation fails">
        <div class="sectionText">
            <h4>I checked generation quality manually</h4>
            <span>During the pilot, generation was not fully reliable: the model could suggest poor wording, make mistakes, or return a result that needed to be adapted to the real work context.</span>
            <span>So I checked quality manually: I tested prompts across different scenarios, reviewed generated plans, and gradually refined the input format and response structure. The interface included loading and error states, as well as a warning to review the plan before using it.</span>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="sectionSeparator sectionSeparatorGray"></div>

    <div class="sectionTextAndImageRight">
        <div class="sectionTextAndImageText sectionTextTwoThird">
            <h4>Results and insights</h4>
            <span>Development took two sprints. Designing and testing the AI logic started earlier and required more time. By July 2025, the AI plan was already working.</span>
            <span>The main insight: the free-form scenario was more useful than automated skill analysis. People needed a structure for their own thoughts more than a formal way to close a skill gap.</span>
            <h4 class="aiIdpSectionGap">IDP coverage increased</h4>
            <span>The funnel: more than 1,000 users opened the scenario, about 40% tried to generate goals, and around 20% of them added the result to their plan.</span>
            <span>Development plan coverage grew to 42.82% in 2026, compared with 31.34% the year before. The AI plan supported this growth by lowering the entry threshold and giving employees a ready structure.</span>
            <span>Other projects also affected the metric: the IDP redesign itself and meetups. The AI plan supported coverage growth and attracted an audience, but its exact contribution to the total number still needs to be measured.</span>
        </div>
